{%- comment -%}
  The loading state, shared by anything that has to wait: the quick view while
  the piece is fetched, the search overlay while a query is in flight, and
  whatever comes next. One mark, so a wait always looks the same.

  The mark is a **round brilliant in elevation**, drawn to a reference given on
  request: the table across the top, the crown broken into five facets, the
  girdle across the widest points, and the pavilion converging on the culet.
  Every vertex is stated once and the lines are grouped by depth, which is what
  lets the light cross the stone rather than arrive on all of it at once.

  It replaced the design's own three-path cut — the one it draws in its image
  placeholders. That mark is right where it lives, at 100px behind a photograph
  that has not loaded, and too plain at 68px in the middle of an empty panel,
  which is what a wait actually shows. The design states no loading state
  anywhere in its 24 pages, so both the shape and the movement here are the
  theme's; what is kept is its language — hairline, gold, square-on.

  What it does is draw itself: the outline is struck in one stroke, the facets
  catch the light once it closes, then the inner facets, girdle and outline
  disappear in that order so it can be struck again. `pathLength="100"` is
  what makes the draw possible without measuring anything in JavaScript — it
  normalises the outline to 100 units, so one `stroke-dasharray` in the
  stylesheet drives it.

  Takes:
    label  what a screen reader announces. Defaults to `general.loading`.
    size   `sm`, `md` (default) or `lg`.

  It carries `role="status"`, so the label is announced when it appears — and
  it must therefore be *rendered into* the page at the moment of waiting rather
  than sitting there hidden, or nothing is announced at all.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign loader_label = label
  if loader_label == blank
    assign loader_label = 'general.loading' | t
  endif

  assign loader_size = size | default: 'md'
-%}

<span class="loader loader--{{ loader_size }}" role="status">
  {%- comment -%}
    Every line is its own path, and that is not tidiness: `pathLength="100"`
    normalises a path's *total* length, so several lines sharing one path would
    each get a share of that 100 in proportion to how long they are — the
    girdle would still be halfway drawn while a crown facet a third its length
    had long finished. One path per line means one pen speed for all of them.

    `--n` is the order they are struck in, and pairs share a number so the mark
    is symmetric in every frame: the cut, the girdle, the crown from the middle
    out, then the pavilion. `--exit` names the fade timeline that compensates
    for that draw delay, letting all the inner facets disappear together before
    the girdle and outline without making the next loop flash.

    The stroke is deliberately **not** `non-scaling-stroke`, however much a
    hairline that ignores scale sounds right here. It defeats the draw: the dash
    is then measured in device space, so `pathLength`'s normalisation no longer
    governs it and the same 100-unit dash paints an identical row of marching
    dashes at every offset. Measured — 534 inked pixels at offset 0, 50 and 100
    alike, against 1869 / 932 / 0 with an ordinary stroke. The width is scaled
    per size in the stylesheet instead.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    comment
      The mark is a theme setting — Theme settings → Loading Animation. Every
      variant reuses the same classes, pathLength contract, and exit keyframes,
      so the draw engine, the stagger, and reduced motion apply unchanged. An
      unknown stored value falls back to the diamond rather than to nothing.
    endcomment
    case settings.loader_style
      when 'ring'
        render 'icon', icon: 'loader-ring'
      when 'orbit'
        render 'icon', icon: 'loader-orbit'
      else
        render 'icon', icon: 'loader-mark'
    endcase
  -%}
  <span class="visually-hidden">{{ loader_label }}</span>
</span>
